Enjoy this small glimpse of the taonga held within Auckland Libraries Heritage Collections, available to explore via Kura Heritage Collections Online.&nbsp;
These photographs show a range of musical moments in Tāmaki Makaurau. From a school music festival in Manukau to a dance band at the Easter Show, The Keils Isles at the Jive Centre to Upper Hutt Posse on Mount Street. &nbsp;
Together they highlight the diversity of the Tāmaki music scene.&nbsp;
If what you see sparks your curiosity we invite you to discover more in the Encore! exhibition at the Central City Library.&nbsp;
Encore! celebrates how live music brings people together through collective experience. The exhibition looks at ways connection happens through live music. Being emotionally moved by a performance, connections between those on stage and those in the crowd, and the feeling of being connected to others in a music community.&nbsp;

Read and walk!
Ōrewa Library will have their first StoryWalk® for the community to enjoy, to commemorate Anzac Day.
What is a StoryWalk®?
An outdoor picture book!
Follow the story around the park and read along with your whānau and friends.
The StoryWalk® Project was created by Anne Ferguson and developed in collaboration with the Kellogg-Hubbard StoryWalk® Library in Montpelier Vermont.&nbsp;
StoryWalk® is a registered service mark owned by the Kellogg-Hubbard Library.&nbsp;
Our thanks to Scholastic for permission to use the book, This is Where I Stand, written by Philippa Werry and illustrated by Kieran Rynhart.&nbsp;

All Together Now singing opportunities for 2026&nbsp;
This year we are offering more choices than ever before in our quest to provide choirs that meet a range of singers' needs. For the first time, we will offer the new concept, Singing Club. We are also opening our Project Choir, a semi-professional performance ensemble, to experienced members of our main Concert Choirs.&nbsp;
Each term ranges from 8 to 10 weeks of rehearsals plus any performances.&nbsp;
Concert Choirs&nbsp;
These choirs are currently all located in Auckland. They are the choirs that provide the main performance opportunities for those who would like to participate in our concerts or other more formal performances. While performance is never mandatory, our expectation is that members of these choirs will prepare to sing the entire concert in the Auckland Town Hall on Saturday 21 November 2026.
This choir is currently full. We have a waiting list but recommend you select another choir in the meantime.

Venue: Main Hall, Grey Lynn Community Centre, 510 Richmond Road, Grey Lynn
Time: Wednesday, 7.15pm - 9.15pm
Choir Leaders: Steven Rapana and Denzel Panama
Venue: Main Hall, Grey Lynn Community Centre, 510 Richmond Road, Grey Lynn
